17. Five Criteria for Test Automation
Criterion 1: Frequency of Execution

Explanation: Tests that are run frequently (multiple times per day/week) are excellent candidates for automation. Manual execution of frequent tests is time-consuming and error-prone.

Application to Test Scenario:
Test: "POST /api/courses/ endpoint returns 201 with correct course data when valid input is provided"

This test will be executed:

    After every code change (developer testing)

    During CI/CD pipeline (build verification)

    During regression testing (before releases)

    Frequency: 20-50 times per week

Decision: AUTOMATE - High frequency makes this ideal for automation.
Criterion 2: Risk of Manual Error

Explanation: Tests with complex steps, multiple data points, or precise validation requirements are prone to human error when performed manually.

Application to Test Scenario:
Test: "POST /api/courses/ endpoint returns 201 with correct course data when valid input is provided"

Manual testing risks:

    Forgetting to validate all response fields

    Missing the Location header check

    Not verifying database state

    Inconsistent test data between runs

Decision: AUTOMATE - High risk of manual error makes automation preferable.
Criterion 3: Time Required for Manual Execution

Explanation: Tests that take significant time to execute manually (especially repeated tests) benefit greatly from automation.

Application to Test Scenario:
Test: "POST /api/courses/ endpoint returns 201 with correct course data when valid input is provided"

Manual execution time breakdown:

    Setup test data (create department): 5 minutes

    Execute API call: 1 minute

    Validate response: 3 minutes

    Verify database: 2 minutes

    Teardown: 2 minutes
    Total: ~13 minutes per execution

Automated execution time: ~2 seconds

Decision: AUTOMATE - 13 minutes vs 2 seconds is huge time savings.
Criterion 4: Data-Driven Requirements

Explanation: Tests that need to run with multiple data sets (different inputs and expected outputs) are much more efficient when automated.

Application to Test Scenario:
Test: "POST /api/courses/ endpoint returns 201 with correct course data when valid input is provided"

Data variations needed:

    Different course names (various lengths, special characters)

    Different codes (uppercase, lowercase, alphanumeric)

    Different credits (1-6)

    Different department IDs

    With and without descriptions

Decision: AUTOMATE - Multiple data combinations make automation efficient.
Criterion 5: Value of Regression Coverage

Explanation: Tests that protect against regressions in critical business logic should be automated to ensure consistent validation after changes.

Application to Test Scenario:
Test: "POST /api/courses/ endpoint returns 201 with correct course data when valid input is provided"

Critical business value:

    Course creation is a core feature

    Failure would block all users

    Affects enrollment, student records, etc.

    Regression would be highly visible

Decision: AUTOMATE - High business value and regression importance.

Final Decision: This test should be AUTOMATED based on all five criteria.
18. Test Case Automation Decisions
a) Regression test for all CRUD endpoints after every code change

Decision: AUTOMATE

Justification:

    High frequency: Runs after every code change (20+ times/week)

    Critical business logic: CRUD operations are core functionality

    Large scope: Multiple endpoints (Create, Read, Update, Delete)

    Time-consuming: Manual regression takes hours

    Repetitive: Same tests run repeatedly

    Risk reduction: Catches regressions early

Implementation: Create a regression suite that runs automatically in CI/CD pipeline on every push.
b) Exploratory testing of a new search feature

Decision: MANUAL

Justification:

    Unknown behavior: Feature is new and behavior may change

    Unclear expected results: Need human judgment to evaluate relevance

    Creative testing: Testers need to think of edge cases dynamically

    UI/UX evaluation: Need human perspective on usability

    Rapid changes: Feature is in development, UI/behavior changes frequently

    One-time: Exploratory testing is done once per feature

Implementation: Manual test session with a checklist of areas to explore.
c) Performance test: 100 concurrent users calling GET /api/v1/courses/

Decision: AUTOMATE

Justification:

    Precise measurement: Need consistent, repeatable metrics

    Requires tools: JMeter, k6, or similar load testing tools

    Data-driven: Need to test with different concurrency levels

    Repetitive: Run before every release

    Time-critical: Manual performance testing is impossible

    Trend analysis: Need historical data to track performance over time

Implementation: JMeter or k6 script that runs against staging environment.
d) UI test for the login form

Decision: AUTOMATE

Justification:

    Repetitive: Login is needed for many tests

    Critical security: Authentication is high-risk area

    Multiple scenarios: Valid login, invalid login, locked account, etc.

    Regression prone: Login can break easily with UI changes

    Foundation: Login is prerequisite for many other tests

    ROI: High ROI due to frequent execution

Implementation: Selenium WebDriver test that verifies login functionality.
e) Verify the API documentation (Swagger) is accurate

Decision: MANUAL

Justification:

    Content validation: Needs human judgment for accuracy

    Language quality: Grammar, spelling, clarity assessment

    Business accuracy: Verify descriptions match business requirements

    Formatting: Check layout and readability

    Low frequency: Updated when API changes

    Subjective: "Good" documentation is subjective

Implementation: Manual review by QA or technical writer during release preparation.
f) Smoke test: verify the API is reachable after deployment

Decision: AUTOMATE

Justification:

    High frequency: Runs on every deployment

    Critical: Verifies deployment was successful

    Simple: Easy to automate (just GET request to /health)

    Fast: Executes in < 5 seconds

    Early detection: Catches deployment issues immediately

    Reliable: Can run in CI/CD pipeline post-deployment

Implementation: Simple script that calls health endpoint and validates response.
19. Test Automation ROI Calculation
Problem: Calculating Automation ROI

Given:

    Automation cost: 4 hours (to write and debug the automated test)

    Manual execution time: 30 minutes (0.5 hours per run)

    Maintenance overhead: 20% per run after the 10th run

Initial Investment
text

Initial Automation Cost = 4 hours

Cost Without Automation (Manual)
text

Manual Cost per Run = 0.5 hours
Total Manual Cost for N Runs = N * 0.5 hours

Cost With Automation
text

Automation Cost = 4 hours (one-time)
Maintenance Cost = 20% of (0.5 hours) = 0.1 hours per run after 10th run
Total Automated Cost for N Runs = 4 + (max(0, N-10) * 0.1)

Break-Even Point

We need to find N where:
text

Manual Cost = Automated Cost
N * 0.5 = 4 + (N-10) * 0.1  [for N > 10]
N * 0.5 = 4 + 0.1N - 1
N * 0.5 = 3 + 0.1N
0.4N = 3
N = 7.5

Since the formula with maintenance only applies after 10 runs, let's check both cases:

Case 1: N <= 10
text

N * 0.5 = 4
N = 8 runs

Case 2: N > 10
text

N * 0.5 = 4 + (N-10) * 0.1
0.5N = 4 + 0.1N - 1
0.4N = 3
N = 7.5 runs (not applicable since N > 10)
